--TEST--
`Core\Str::padStart` and `::padEnd` cut one and the same run at the run's own end, and place it on opposite sides of a subject they never otherwise touch — spec § 1, ADR 0009 § 2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The two members share `padding_run` and `write_run`, so what is worth
// asserting is not a row of either one's output — `str-pad-members.nvst` has
// those — but that the run is a *function of the shortfall alone*. Written as
// an agreement over a sweep: whatever the subject, the padding a member adds
// must be exactly what the same member gives an **empty** subject asked for
// the shortfall, and the two members must add the identical run.
//
// That is a stronger question than either member's own line answers. A member
// that cut the run at the end nearest the subject rather than at the run's own
// end still reads plausibly beside its arguments — `padEnd("ab", 7, "xy")` is
// `"abxyxyx"`, whose dropped piece is the *far* end, and `padStart` drops the
// near one, because both cut what `write_run` writes last. Only asking both of
// them the same question catches a member that grew its own direction.
final class Run {
    /// Whether both members place, on their own side, exactly the run an empty
    /// subject is given for the same shortfall — and reach the width doing it.
    public static function agrees(string $subject, uint $width, string $padding): int {
        uint $have = Core\Str::length($subject);
        uint $want = $width;
        if ($have > $want) {
            $want = $have;
        }
        string $run = Core\Str::padStart("", $want - $have, $padding);
        string $before = Core\Str::padStart($subject, $width, $padding);
        string $after = Core\Str::padEnd($subject, $width, $padding);
        if ($before != $run . $subject) {
            return 0;
        }
        if ($after != $subject . $run) {
            return 0;
        }
        // Counted in ADR 0009 § 2's unit, which is what makes the row below
        // with a combining mark in the padding an assertion and not a
        // coincidence: a run cut mid-cluster would leave a bare combining mark
        // that this length does not count, and the width would come up short.
        if (Core\Str::length($before) != $want) {
            return 0;
        }
        if (Core\Str::length($after) != $want) {
            return 0;
        }
        return 1;
    }
}

// Three subjects and three paddings, none of whose lengths divide the others,
// so most of the sweep's cells cut the run partway through a copy. The
// two-piece padding whose first piece is `e` + a combining acute is the one
// that makes the cut a cluster boundary rather than a byte offset.
array<string> $subjects = ["", "x", "abcd"];
array<string> $paddings = ["-", "xy", "e\u{301}n", "abc"];

int $agreed = 0;
int $asked = 0;
uint $one = 1;
uint $width = 0;
uint $widths = 9;
foreach ($subjects as string $subject) {
    foreach ($paddings as string $padding) {
        $width = 0;
        while ($width < $widths) {
            $asked = $asked + 1;
            $agreed = $agreed + Run::agrees($subject, $width, $padding);
            $width = $width + $one;
        }
    }
}
echo "both place the shortfall's own run: ", $agreed, " of ", $asked, "\n";

// The same fact said once without the sweep, because the sweep proves it and
// this shows it: an empty subject gets the identical string from both members,
// and it is whole pieces of the padding however the shortfall falls.
echo "[", Core\Str::padStart("", 5, "e\u{301}n"), "][", Core\Str::padEnd("", 5, "e\u{301}n"), "]\n";

// And the cut is at the run's own end on both sides, which is the one place
// the two look different: the piece `padStart` drops abuts the subject, the
// piece `padEnd` drops ends the result.
echo "[", Core\Str::padStart("x", 3, "xyz"), "][", Core\Str::padEnd("x", 3, "xyz"), "]\n";
--EXPECT--
both place the shortfall's own run: 108 of 108
[énéné][énéné]
[xyx][xxy]
